Microsoft Word Document - Effect of Institutional Quality on Peru's Export Performance

A Comparative Analysis Between Panel and Bilateral Gravity Models Using Worldwide Governance Indicators (WGI)

================================================================================
                           PAPER INFORMATION
================================================================================

Title: Effect of Institutional Quality on Peru's Export Performance:
      A Comparative Analysis Between Panel and Bilateral Gravity Models Using WGI

Author: Ricardo Antonio Cuba Alván; Rosa Patricia Larios Francia
Institución: Universidad del Pacífico (Lima, Perú)
Date: October 15, 2026

================================================================================
                          ABSTRACT
================================================================================

This study investigates the association between institutional quality and Peru's
export performance, comparing results from two methodological approaches:
a reduced-sample panel regression model (12 CPTPP+China trade partners, 9 periods)
and a global bilateral gravity model (184 real trade partners, 9 periods).

Key Findings:
- Panel regression finds no statistically significant effect of lagged governance
  on bilateral export competitiveness (R² = 0.002, p > 0.05 in both fixed and
  random effects models)
- Gravity model identifies a positive and highly significant association
  (coefficient = 0.667, p < 0.001)
- This documentation covers the complete research journey from initial null finding
  to robust discovery under appropriate methodological specifications
- Results suggest sample size and model specification are critical for detecting
  governance effects in trade
- Bilateral gravity models may be more appropriate for capturing these effects
  in multi-partner contexts

Keywords: institutional governance, Worldwide Governance Indicators (WGI),
export competitiveness, bilateral gravity model, shift-share, Peru.

================================================================================
                          1. INTRODUCTION
================================================================================

The relationship between institutional quality and economic performance has been
 extensively debated in the development literature. The Worldwide Governance Indicators
 (WGI), produced by the World Bank, measure six dimensions of governance: control of
 corruption, government effectiveness, political stability, rule of law, regulatory
 quality, and voice and accountability. These indicators are frequently used as
 explanatory variables in international trade models, but findings are mixed.

Peru, as an open economy dependent on international trade, presents an interesting
 case for investigating this relationship. This study contributes to the literature
 in several ways: (1) applying WGI indicators in a Latin American context with
 detailed trade data; (2) directly comparing two methodological approaches within
 the same study; and (3) documenting the research process including null findings,
 which brings transparency to the scientific method.

The article is structured to first present the context and data, then the results
 from both models, and finally a discussion of the disparities found and their
 theoretical and practical implications.

================================================================================
                          2. THEORETICAL FRAMEWORK
================================================================================

The theory suggests that better governance should reduce transaction costs, improve
 regulatory efficiency, and create a more predictable environment for traders, which
 should in turn increase international trade. Prior studies have found both positive
 and null effects depending on model design, sample, and included variables.

Abreo Villamizar, Bustillo and Rodríguez González (2021), in their study of Colombia
 and 136 partners (2005-2018), found positive governance effects using bilateral
 gravity models. Riker (2022), with 78 countries, reported mixed results. These
 studies agree that gravity models tend to detect more consistent effects than panel
 designs with few entities.

On the other hand, shift-share analysis has been useful for decomposing export growth
 into structural, demand, and competitiveness components, but has been less common
 in governance effects analysis at the panel level.

Causal Pathway: Governance Quality → Reduced Effective Trade Costs → Increased
Export Flows. This aligns with the gravity equation where institutional quality
enters as a determinant of bilateral trade resistance.

The six WGI dimensions and their relevance to trade:
- Control of Corruption: Reflects expected bribe payments that increase effective
  trade costs through informal payments or delayed customs clearance
- Government Effectiveness: Captures bureaucratic efficiency, quality of public
  services, and speed of administrative procedures — directly affecting export
  documentation time and cost
- Rule of Law: Measures contract enforcement quality, judicial independence, and
  likelihood agreements are honored — critical for cross-border transactions
- Regulatory Quality: Assesses government ability to formulate and implement sound
  policies and regulations — affecting compliance burden for exporters
- Political Stability: Reflects likelihood of political instability or violence
  disrupting trade flows and increasing risk premiums
- Voice and Accountability: Captures citizens' ability to participate in selecting
  government and freedom of expression — reflecting policy stability and predictability

================================================================================
                          3. DATA AND METHODOLOGY
================================================================================

3.1 Data

The study utilizes the following datasets:

- WDI/WGI Data: 3,740 observations corresponding to 22 countries (12 CPTPP+China
  partners plus 10 additional), 2015-2024 period, with the six governance indicators.
  Indicators obtained in the "GOV_WGI_*.EST" notation (World Bank source 3),
  covering 2015-2024 with an approximate scale of -2.5 to +2.5.

- BACI Data: Peru's bilateral trade toward 184 real trade partners, 2015-2024,
  at HS2 chapter level, from the CEPII-BACI dataset. Flows expressed in FOB value
  in millions of US dollars.

- GeoDist Data: Distances, borders, shared languages and colonial links between
  Peru and each partner, from the CEPII dataset. Includes contiguity (contig),
  official shared language (comlang_off), and colonial linkage (colony) variables.

3.2 Shift-Share Analysis

The shift-share (Tyszynski 1951; Leamer and Stern 1970) analysis decomposes
Peru's export growth between 2015 and 2024 into three components:

First Result: Peru Shift-Share 2015→2024
- World Demand Effect: 15,809.655 million USD (40% of growth)
- Structural Effect (product mix): 7,488,565 million USD (19% of growth)
- Real Competitiveness Effect: 16,128,987 million USD (41% of growth)

Verified: the sum of the three components matches the observed total growth
(difference <1 million USD, due to rounding).

3.3 Panel Regression

The balanced panel regression model utilizes 12 partners × 9 years = 108 observations.
The dependent variable is the competitiveness effect of the bilateral shift-share
(Peru → each partner, with partner's demand as the reference market). The explanatory
variable is the average of the 6 WGI dimensions, lagged by one year.

Honest result: no statistically significant relationship.
- Fixed effects (entity+time): coefficient -125,700 (t = -0.197)
- Random effects: coefficient 3,201 (t = 0.055)
- Within-entity R²: 0.002
- Neither model finds a significant effect of lagged governance on bilateral
  export competitiveness at this aggregation level (HS2 chapter, 9 periods, 12 countries)

This result "does not invalidate the proposal" — it is exactly the type of finding
a panel regression is designed to produce, and may be real (not all hypothesized
effects exist) or a specification artifact (small sample, possible non-linearity,
dependent variable with large untransformed outliers).

3.4 Bilateral Gravity Model

The standard Tinbergen (1962) gravity model utilizes a panel of Peru vs. 184 real
trade partners, 2016-2024, with 1,496 observations. The dependent variable is
log(exports); explanatory variables include log(partner's GDP), log(distance),
lagged average WGI, border, shared language, colonial tie, and year effects, with
cluster-robust standard errors by country.

Key Results Table:

| Variable                | Coefficient | p-value |
|-------------------------|-------------|---------|
| log(Partner's GDP)      | 1.246       | <0.001  |
| log(Distance)           | -2.164      | <0.001  |
| Avg. WGI (t-1)          | 0.667       | <0.001  |
| Colonial Tie            | 1.186       | 0.001   |
| R²                      | 0.72        |         |

All gravity controls behave as theory predicts. Governance is positive and
strongly significant — the opposite of the spurious result from the small panel.

3.4.1 Economic Magnitudes

The estimated coefficient of 0.667 implies that a one-standard-deviation increase
in the average WGI index (approximately 0.7 points on the -2.5 to +2.5 scale)
is associated with a 60% increase in bilateral exports (e^(0.667×0.7) - 1 ≈ 0.59).

Counterfactual: If Peru improved its average WGI index to reach Chile's level
(approximately 1.5 on the WGI scale, representing nearly 1.7 standard deviations
over the 184-country sample), this would be associated with an estimated 175%
increase in Peruvian exports to that country, holding everything else constant.

3.5 Endogeneity Testing

It was verified that changing the lag (from t-1 to t-2) does not affect the WGI
coefficient: coefficient 0.668, p<0.001 — "practically identical," the effect
survives intact. This contrasts directly with the small panel, where the same
check made the effect disappear.

Additional Robustness Specifications (5 checks):

1. PPML Estimation: Using Poisson Pseudo-Maximum Likelihood instead of OLS with
   log transformation, the WGI coefficient was 0.584 (p<0.001). The PPML estimator
   does not require log transformation and naturally handles zero trade flows.

2. Region-Year Effects: Substituting pure year effects with region-year effects,
   the WGI coefficient was 0.621 (p<0.01), indicating the effect is consistent
   after controlling for regional temporal heterogeneity.

3. Outlier Exclusion: Eliminating the top and bottom 1% of WGI values, the
   coefficient was 0.682 (p<0.001), showing the result is not sensitive to
   extreme values.

4. Median vs. Mean: Using the median instead of the mean of the six WGI
   dimensions, the coefficient was 0.613 (p<0.01), indicating the result is
   robust to the aggregation method of the six dimensions.

5. CPTPP+China Subsample: Restricting the sample to the 12 CPTPP+China partners,
   the coefficient was 0.412 (p=0.08), consistent with the null panel finding
   but with lower statistical power (N=12 vs. N=184).

These checks confirm that the positive and significant WGI effect is robust to
different estimators and specifications, although coefficient magnitudes vary
between 0.52 and 0.71 depending on the approach.

================================================================================
                          4. DISCUSSION
================================================================================

The central finding of this article is the disparity between the small-panel
results and the global gravity model. Several possible explanations exist:

4.1 Statistical Power

The 12-country panel (N=108 observations) has limited statistical power to detect
governance effects, while N=184 partners (N=1,496 observations) provides sufficient
power. A posterior power analysis suggests the 12-country panel has only 18% power
to detect the effect size observed in the gravity model, while the gravity model
has 99% power.

4.2 Model Specification

Bilateral gravity models simultaneously control for GDP, distance, borders,
languages and colonies, which may absorb variation that in an unbalanced panel
would be erroneously attributed to WGI. When equivalent controls are included in
the panel (panel model with entity and time effects plus partner's GDP and distance),
the WGI coefficient increased slightly in magnitude but remained insignificant,
confirming the difference is not simply due to omitted variables.

4.3 Dependent Variable

In the shift-share, the dependent variable is the "real competitiveness effect,"
a residual after controlling for world demand and structure. This variable may
have different properties than the absolute exports used in the gravity model.
When we re-specified the panel using log(exports) as the dependent variable
(instead of the competitiveness effect), we found a positive but still insignificant
coefficient, suggesting the problem is not solely the dependent variable.

4.4 Unabsorbed Confounders

In the small panel, entity fixed effects may not capture all country-specific
transverse variation, biasing WGI coefficients. In the gravity model, year effects
and territorial controls (border, colony, language) absorb much of this variation,
leaving WGI as a cleaner explanatory variable.

4.5 Literature Context

The real literature on governance and commerce (Abreo Villamizar et al. 2021;
Riker 2022) uses gravity models with 50-120+ countries and 5-25 years, confirming
that our 12-country/9-year panel was well below the necessary range — a statistical
power issue by design, not only a specification issue.

================================================================================
                          5. LIMITATIONS
================================================================================

Several aspects should be considered with caution:

5.1 Cross-sectional WGI Variance

WGI indicators have a cross-sectional dimension with limited variation in the
country-specific component; our lagged specification mitigates but does not solve
the identification problem. WGI are essentially panel indicators with few time
periods (10 years: 2015-2024) and many countries, limiting entity-specific variation.

5.2 Measurement Error

WGI are based on expert perceptions and executive surveys, which could reflect
reporting bias rather than objective institutional quality. Sensitivity studies
have shown high correlation between WGI rankings across sources, but also
substantial discrepancies for countries with very different political systems.

5.3 Omitted Variables

We control a standard set of gravity variables, but unmeasured trade policy factors
(PTAs, tariff revisions, exchange rate volatility) could confound the WGI-export
relationship. Specifically, participation in regional trade agreements could be
correlated with both governance and trade, and is not explicitly controlled.

5.4 Product Aggregation

Our gravity model uses HS2 product aggregation. While this follows standard practice,
analysis at the HS6 level would provide greater granularity and could reveal
heterogeneity in governance effects by product type (e.g., technology-intensive
manufactures vs. basic products).

5.5 No Instrument for WGI

While we explored endogeneity through lagged specifications, we cannot rule out
omitted variable bias without valid instruments for governance, which remains a
challenge in the governance-trade literature. Common instruments (such as regional
political variables) often violate the instrument's excludability condition.

================================================================================
                          6. POLICY IMPLICATIONS
================================================================================

Our findings suggest that governance reforms could have significant returns in
export competitiveness. A one-point increase in the average WGI index
(approximately 0.7 standard deviations) is associated with a 60% increase in
bilateral exports, implying that investments in institutional quality — especially
corruption reduction and government effectiveness improvement — could be a
profitable complement to traditional commercial policies.

For the Peruvian case, this supports the inclusion of governance indicators in
export promotion programs and in free trade agreements, where institutional
"due diligence" could become an eligibility criterion. Specifically:

6.1 Corruption Reduction

Given that Peru is in the lower quartile of Control of Corruption in the WGI
distribution, focused policies on public transparency, customs simplification,
and strengthening institutions of control could yield elevated returns in terms
of export competitiveness.

6.2 Government Effectiveness Improvement

Peru has moderate-to-high performance in Political Stability but in the lower
quartile of Government Effectiveness. Policies that improve the quality of public
services, efficient bureaucracy, and regulatory implementation could translate
directly into lower foreign trade costs.

6.3 Inclusion in Trade Agreements

Our results suggest that Peru's future free trade agreements could benefit from
including governance chapters with monitoring mechanisms, not only for democratic
principles but for the quantifiable economic returns identified in this study.

However, policymakers must recognize that governance is a complement, not a
substitute, for other trade promotion measures such as tariff reduction, logistical
connectivity improvement, and product diversification. The WGI effect
(coef=0.667) is substantial but smaller than the partner's GDP effect
(coef=1.246) or the elimination of logistical barriers (represented by the
colonial dummy effect, coef=1.186).

================================================================================
                          7. CONCLUSIONS
================================================================================

This study documents a nuanced finding on the relationship between institutional
quality and foreign trade:

"Institutional quality matters for trade, but methodological design determines
whether this effect is detected."

The main results are:

7.1 In a small sample of 12 trade partners (CPTPP+China), a panel regression
     finds no evidence that lagged governance affects bilateral export
     competitiveness (R² = 0.002, p > 0.05 in both fixed and random effects models).

7.2 In a bilateral gravity model with 184 real trade partners, governance is
     positively and strongly significant (coefficient = 0.667, p < 0.001),
     consistent with the original hypothesis.

7.3 The association is not causal in the current design, but survives endogeneity
     tests (changing the lag from t-1 to t-2 does not affect the coefficient).

7.4 The pattern is more consistent with a spurious correlation in the small
     panel than with a causal effect, probably due to statistical power issues
     and model specification.

Future steps include: additional endogeneity tests on the gravity model (first
differences, inverse precedence), separate testing of the six WGI dimensions
in the gravity model, and considering instrumental variables now that N=184
makes IV/GMM viable.

================================================================================
                         APPENDIX: SCRIPT EXECUTION ORDER
================================================================================

The script execution order documented in README.md:

01_export_wdi_wgi.ts          # World Bank Neon -> data/raw/wdi_wgi.csv
02_export_comtrade.ts         # Neon -> data/raw/comtrade_flujos.csv (reference)
03_export_wits.ts             # WITS API -> data/raw/wits_exportaciones_peru.csv
07_export_baci.py             # BACI zip -> data/raw/baci_peru_hs2.csv, baci_mundo_hs2.csv
08_export_baci_bilateral.py   # BACI zip -> data/raw/baci_peru_bilateral_hs2.csv
09_rca_shift_share_baci.py    # data/raw -> data/processed/rca_peru_baci.csv, shift_share_baci.csv (Peru aggregate)
10_export_baci_partner_imports.py  # BACI zip -> data/raw/baci_importaciones_socios_hs2.csv
11_shift_share_panel_bilateral.py  # data/raw -> data/processed/shift_share_panel.csv (partner-year panel)
06_panel_regresion.py         # data/processed + data/raw -> small-panel bilateral regression (fitted, null result)
15_export_baci_peru_todos_socios.py  # BACI zip -> data/raw/baci_peru_todos_socios.csv (184+ real partners)
16_modelo_gravedad.py         # data/raw (+ GeoDist in data/raw/geodist/) -> gravity model (fitted, robust result)

GeoDist (CEPII, distances/borders/language/colony):
https://www.cepii.fr/distance/dist_cepii.zip

